# The errors PLRM 8.2 gives each operator, and what this interpreter
# answers when the one that can be asked mechanically is asked.
#
# DERIVED FROM THE SPECIFICATION, not from this interpreter, which is the
# whole of what makes it worth comparing against: it is the side of the
# comparison xpost cannot move.
#
# ---- how it was derived, so it can be derived again
#
# From the text of the specification (a PDF-to-text rendering of PLRM 3rd
# edition), by reading each operator's entry in section 8.2. An entry
# opens with the operator's name on a line of its own, then its syntax
# line -- the same name among the operands it takes and the results it
# leaves -- and ends with an Errors: list and a See Also: line.
#
# The name/syntax pair is what has to be anchored on, and getting that
# wrong is the trap this file exists past. A name is printed in many
# places besides its own entry: an example that ends in it, a
# cross-reference, a running head. Each of those is followed by some
# OTHER entry's Errors: list, so a scan that takes the next Errors: after
# any occurrence of the name reports a neighbour's errors and looks
# entirely plausible doing it. Read that way, closepath comes back with
# arc's four errors and mark with a list belonging to whatever entry
# follows the example it appears in.
#
# Three tests together pick the entry out. Its name line is preceded by
# the previous entry's See Also:; its syntax line is formulaic -- short,
# no sentence punctuation, no word that belongs to prose rather than to a
# list of operands; and the Errors: list is the first after that line. Of
# the 382 operators the specification defines, 380 resolve to exactly one
# entry that way and none to more than one.
#
# The generator is not kept in the tree and the specification text is not
# either: neither is something a checkout has, so a gate that read them
# would answer differently on a machine that happened to have a copy. The
# table is data here, and the paragraph above is what regenerates it.
#
# ---- the lines
#
#   errors  <name>  <the errors the specification lists, or - for none>
#   nosuch  <name>  <why the specification text yields no list for it>
#   procset <name>  <why a bare call does not answer stackunderflow>
#   answers <n>     how many operators answer stackunderflow when asked
#
# tests/check-plrm-errors.sh asks a running interpreter for one of these
# errors -- stackunderflow, the one that can be provoked without knowing
# anything about an operator beyond its name, by calling it with an empty
# operand stack. Every operator whose specification list carries
# stackunderflow must answer stackunderflow, and every operator excluded
# from that must NOT: an exclusion that starts answering correctly is one
# that has outlived its reason.
#
# The other error classes are not asked. typecheck would need an operand
# of a wrong type per operator, and which type is wrong is per-operator
# knowledge this file does not carry; the errors are recorded all the
# same, because the table is the specification's side and a later gate
# that can drive another class has it to read.

